  "source_text": "Clive Barrington's media empire had received direct payments from Polk's lobbying network, sixty-eight million over five years, in exchange for editorial coordination on stories affecting the council's interests. The payments were disguised as advertising revenue. The editorial directives were documented in internal emails.\n\nAnd Harrison Polk himself, the Broker, the connective tissue, had managed a lobbying budget of one-point-two billion dollars over the preceding decade, every cent traceable, every expenditure linked to a specific political outcome, every outcome linked to a specific benefit for a specific council member.\n\nSophie's tools published the data in real-time: interactive visualizations, searchable databases, downloadable spreadsheets, all flowing to the Scaffold's distribution network. Within minutes, anyone in the world with an internet connection could trace the flow of money from oligarch to senator. Could see the price tag on their democracy. Could calculate, to the penny, how much it had cost to buy their government.\n\nSophie poured herself a glass of wine. A good one. A 2018 Burgundy she had been saving for a moment that deserved it.\n\nShe raised the glass to her laptop screen, where the money trails were branching and multiplying like roots breaking through concrete.\n\n\"I told you I had the receipts,\" she said.\n\nShe watched the numbers populate her visualization tools, twenty years of buried financial records surfacing into daylight, and felt something she had not permitted herself to feel in three decades of professional caution: certainty. Not the careful, qualified certainty of audit reports and compliance reviews. The blinding, dangerous, liberating certainty of a woman who had spent her life suspecting the truth and was now watching it confirm itself, line by line, transaction by transaction, in the cold arithmetic of stolen money.\n\n<short pause>\n\nIn London, Nadia Osei's relay phone went quiet.\n\nThis was the signal. Not a call, not a code word, but the absence of calls. The network she had built — twelve journalists, six countries, forty-seven relay contacts, hundreds of hours of careful cultivation — had been designed with a single indicator of success: silence. When the data went live, the relay chain would stop because there was nothing left to relay. The truth was public. The switchboard could close.\n\nNadia sat in the silence and listened to it. Each quiet second was a node confirming. Each unanswered phone was a journalist receiving data instead of rumors. The silence of success was, she thought, the loudest thing she had ever heard.\n\n<short pause>\n\nSomewhere over the Atlantic, Tomás Ferreira felt his burner phone vibrate once. A single pulse. The pre-arranged signal from the relay chain: live.\n\nHe looked out the airplane window at the dark ocean below and thought about Ana. Twenty-nine years old. Killed in Cambodia, with robbery written on the report. He had told Adrian why he did this. Saying it aloud had not made the anger smaller. The anger was its own explanation, carried in his hands, in his training, in every device he had planted and every door he had opened.\n\nAna, he thought. It's done.\n\nThe phone vibrated once more and went dark. Below him, the Atlantic stretched to the horizon, and somewhere beneath the water, fiber optic cables were carrying the truth from continent to continent at the speed of light.\n\n<short pause>\n\nAt Elias Strutt's corporate headquarters in Menlo Park, the internal security operations center erupted into controlled panic at 3:17 a.m. Pacific time.\n\nEvery alert fired simultaneously. Intrusion detection systems screamed. Data loss prevention tools flagged massive outbound transfers. Access logs showed root-level activity on systems that hadn't been touched in months. The security team, forty-seven analysts on the overnight shift, scrambled to contain a breach that was already over.\n\nThe implants had already opened their channels. The first records were beyond the perimeter; copying and replication continued as the analysts tried to contain them.\n\nIn Austin, Magnus Cole's platform experienced the same cascade of alarms. Cole, who had joined the emergency council call an hour earlier, received a notification on his personal phone and stared at it with the specific expression of a man watching his house burn down while standing in the yard in his bathrobe.",
